A||||22||stsmithgt||Gun safety should start at a young age.||||14||
Z||000000||stsmithgt||12-21-2000||01:23 PM||||It worked for me. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I've practiced gun safety for many years and I haven't killed anyone.  Notice the gun isn't pointed at the camera?  I was 4 years old when I got this BB gun.  Granted there were a few mistakes made.  I shot out a window of a car on the farm.  I didn't watch where the BB was going if I missed the bird.  Ooops!  I wish those gun fearing people would have been brought up being more educated on gun safety then with gun fears.  Scare tactics don't always work.  I think it could even create an obsession toward the object that they are kept away from.  Anyone agree with this point of view?  How old were you when you got your first gun?  any goofs?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstrifle.jpg"> done babbling.||||reg||14
Z||000001||Tigger||12-21-2000||02:20 PM||||No, you can't have a Red Rider BB gun for Christmas... you'll shoot your eye out!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>(stomping) But, Mom...||||reg||8
Z||000002||terryk||12-21-2000||02:29 PM||||Funny you should say that. I passed hunter safety at 7, hunted with my dad and brother until I was about 15 and now I have no desire to own a gun or hunt (I do have the three matching LC Smiths .410/20/12 Ga stored away).<P>I think it took the mystery out of guns. Hunting also gives you plenty of time to balance the affects of a gun with the reality of killing.<P>I agree, do it young. Like me, they may choose to give it up, but I still support it.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||12-21-2000||02:47 PM||||I'm like Terry. I was around guns at a young age. Got my first gun around 8 years old. Did some dumb stuff, never hurt me or anyone, but shot out a car window once, etc. I would always go hunting with dad. I followed and watched, he did the shooting. I was 15 when I got my hunting license. Went hunting for several years while in high-school. Still own my guns(all 4 of them, not many compared to other family members), haven't used them in several years. But, have lost interest in hunting, shooting etc. Cleaning deer is tough, tiring work, and it's always freezing out. <P>"I think it took the mystery out of guns. Hunting also gives you plenty of time to balance the affects of a gun with the reality of killing."  Same thing I was thinking.<P>I am still totally against any sort of gun bans, even the ones currently in action.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||12-21-2000||03:00 PM||||"I don't have to be careful, I have a gun"<P>-Homer Simpson<P><BR>haha <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaRkLoRD||12-21-2000||03:16 PM||||I agree that people should be educated about guns at an early age.. real guns, not ones in movies or videogames.. (I grew up playing games like wolf3d, doom, quake, etc..)<BR>however, I think banning guns from places like schools is still a good idea.. I like being able to go to school and not have to worry about being shot. True, Canada has more restrictive gun laws than the USA... but violent crimes are a lot less common here as well. Take a look at how many school shootings there have been in the USA in the last 3 years.. compare that to the number in Canada.. <P>Some people feel it's their constitutional right to own a weapon.. and that's ok with me. I'd just prefer a society where there was minimal crime in part due to an increased inability to buy guns, to one where everyone on the street was carrying..<P>As for hunting, I have no desire to shoot animals.. since most of us don't need to kill our food, I see no reason to continue to hunt. I'd feel pretty bad about myself if I killed an innocent deer or something, just for fun......<P>sorry for the rant.. I have a close friend who's a "gun nut", and once he told me he considers schools to be "defenseless victim zones" because guns are banned there.. and he'd prefer if every student carried some sort of weapon, to use against anyone else that got violent like the columbine students. I usually don't share his viewpoint, and often have trouble even understanding the logic behind his reasoning.. I think this is one of those times.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000006||p4n1c||12-21-2000||03:31 PM||||Steve, who the h-e-double hockey sticks said that?  Someone from KDSS?<P>Oh yeah, check your email too.  Usually when I email you I have it next second.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>------------------<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'<P>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 12-21-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 12-21-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||12-21-2000||05:31 PM||||yeah, it was someone from KDSS... it was Dan...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>about the email.. resend it to  <A HREF="mailto:blackicefiero@hotmail.com">blackicefiero@hotmail.com</A> <P>(webinbox is down, and I can't access my regular DF account until my machine is working again.)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||BN Boomer||12-21-2000||05:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>Take a look at how many school shootings there have been in the USA in the last 3 years.. compare that to the number in Canada.. <BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I can think of at least 2 or 3 such events that occured in Canada during that time frame. And when the size of each nation's population is taken into consideration, I'll think that you will find the actual rate of incidence to not be as dissimilar as you appear to imply.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>Some people feel it's their constitutional right to own a weapon.. and that's ok with me. I'd just prefer a society where there was minimal crime in part due to an increased inability to buy guns, to one where everyone on the street was carrying..<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So how is restricting the the ability of law abiding citizens to purchase firearms going to deter crime or criminals, who by their very definition do not obey laws? Just look at drugs, they are illegal yet one can probably by any type they can imagine on virtually any high school campus in North America.<P>Furthermore, statistics prove that an armed populace, "where everyone on the street was carrying", is in fact an effective deterent to crime and that crime rates do indeed fall when gun control laws are more relaxed. Look at New York City and Washington DC as prime examples of how effective the strict guntrol measures you seem to support are. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>As for hunting, I have no desire to shoot animals.. since most of us don't need to kill our food, I see no reason to continue to hunt. I'd feel pretty bad about myself if I killed an innocent deer or something, just for fun<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have a really big problem with that statement. First off, it is outrageous for you to imply that hunting is simply killing innocent animals for mere "fun". Most hunters are quite ethical and put what game they take to good use. For us, killing a deer and feeding our families with it is no different than you doing the same thing every time you buy cellophane wrapped meat at the supermarket that appears sterile and devoid of what actually occured so that it may be available to consumers.<P>"Most of us don't need to kill our food"??? Do you eat meat? Where do you think it comes from? That it just falls out of the sky or something like the Biblical manna? Just how is a deer any more innocent than the cow that was raised in captivity and slaughtered so you can eat a Big Mac and have leather seats in your car? Or farm raised chickens that give you eggs or pigs that live a life of misery so you can enjoy cheap ham and bacon? At least that poor, innocent deer was able to enjoy life as nature intended it to before becoming part of the food chain. Can the same be said of all the food sources that you consume? <P>You might feel bad about performing such dirty work yourself, but I suspect that you maintain a clear conscience and sleep pretty well at night even knowing that you just pay someone else to do it for you.<P>As far as guns at schools, I don't know about students, but I'd like to think that we can give options other than bleeding to death to qualified, trained teachers who choose to excerise such options.||||reg||1
Z||000009||p4n1c||12-21-2000||05:58 PM||||Sorry but I can only think of one incident in that time-frame where 'a' kid went in and shot up a school.  That was in Alberta.  The Montreal incident was not performed by a child and I can't think of any third incident off the top of my head.  <P>What I think Steve is implying is that *flame suit on* kids(yes, this is the topic at hand, not shootings in general) shooting up schools is more prominent in the States then in Canada, or if they are prominent up here we are smart enough not to tell anyone to give our country a bad name.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>As for hunting, I too disagree with Steve's statements on that subject.  But that's his own personal opinion and he more then likely just spewed the thoughts out onto the post rather then thinking out and structuring the statement.<P>------------------<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||12-21-2000||05:59 PM||||An armed society is a polite society.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cheever2||12-21-2000||07:10 PM||||There were always guns in my house growing up.  We had 7-9 rifles, 3 shotguns and 3 handguns.  I never ever misused one nor had an accident.  My dad took us target shooting frequently, and did the same in Boy Scouts.  Knew some people who were killed by gun accidents, so maybe that taught me the consequences of what they can do.<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I see no reason to continue to hunt.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>You probably have no idea what hunting does for animal population control.  There are almost no natural predators anymore, like mountain lions and bears, so hunting has a part in correcting that.<BR>-<BR>I have no guns in my own house, only because I don't think I could do as good a job teaching my kids to respect guns like my Dad taught me.  When my boys can do archery without playing William Tell (serious, they  did that once), and when they can have a pocket knife without abusing it, then we'll see about guns.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||DaRkLoRD||12-21-2000||11:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>"Most of us don't need to kill our food"??? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>see what happens when I forget a word? that should have read "Most of don't need to kill our OWN food." <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>As far as guns at schools, I don't know about students, but I'd like to think that we can give options other than bleeding to death to qualified, trained teachers who choose to excerise such options.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>well, I feel safe at my school.. (even considering the neighborhood the college happens to be in...lol) but I doubt I would if I knew that people were carrying weapons in the school. I bet some people do, but because I don't know for sure... I'm not concerned.<P>on the topic of meat though.... I eat a lot less meat than I used to, and if I could buy genetically engineered synthetic meat (we can grow human organs now, why not blocks of pure beef muscle tissue?) I would.. same goes for leather... I'd feel more comfortable using leather products (seat covers, upholstry, gloves, etc) if the material was grown synthetically as opposed to stripping it off a dead cow.. but for some reason, people find this concept to be "unethical", just like growing human organs in a lab...<P>anyway, remind me to never talk about this subject again.... I'm pretty sure I've managed to annoy more than a few people without intending to...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||8
Z||000013||wkayl||12-22-2000||12:11 AM||||I just gotta jump in on this one.  <P>I have hunted off and on for 30 years.  Most of the animals out there owe their life to me.  I can't hit the side of a barn if I was standin inside.  That said, my son and I went on our 1st real hunting trip together.  He is 18.  The last few years we would go out the second weekend of elk season (cuse I never did get one).  I would cary my gun and he would cary a disabled BB gun.  I showed him how to carry a gun and how to respect it.  This year I got my 1st elk.  I was never into sports, but my son is (4 years HS football, many years of baseball). He was nearly a 4.0 student in high school and graduated with honors in the top 15 of a class of over 400.  I made the b honor roll once (you may notice by my spelling).  He is now an engenering student at CU Boulder.  This was the first time we spent together as equals, not Father raising a Son.  I think we became better friends even though we have always had a very good relationship.  I could go on and on about the right to own guns and the things our hunting fees pay for but that argument has been going on for years.  The $300+ that I spent that week was worth it to me, even in the years I never got anything.  This coming week I'm going to be in NE South Dakota hopefully catching some fish through the ice.  It would be cheaper to go to the store and buy some but then I would be just getting something that someone else "killed".<P>Ok, I'm done.....||||reg||1
Z||000014||terryk||12-22-2000||02:02 AM||||We've just subcontracted the killing of our food. We can cancel that contract at anytime. I don't hunt anymore, but if I'm hungry, that deer's dead.<P>All an armed society will do is make frivolous killing feel normal. At the same time, banning guns will have the same affect as banning rats. Illegal, but still there. The only real changes in society come from the commitment to change, not laws to force behavour. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||ur2biteme||12-22-2000||06:40 AM||||i do not own guns, but i was raised around them, brought to shooting ranges, etc.  i dont like guns, but that's a choice i made after being educated about them.  <P>when you ban guns, the only pepole that have them are criminals, therefore making it easier for the criminals to commit their crimes without fear of getting his head blown off by crawling in the wrong window.  all you are doing is taking away rights from law abiding citizens.  <P>the only gun control i want to see is that criminals cant walk into a gun shop or show and buy one, or making sure that the law abiding citizens knew what they were doing with the gun through licensing.  guns dont kill people, murderers do.  the gun is just a tool.  <P>i've said my piece<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000016||DaRkLoRD||12-22-2000||02:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by p4n1c:<BR><B>more then likely just spewed the thoughts out onto the post rather then thinking out and structuring the statement.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>wow, I've *never* done <I>that</I> before....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||11
Z||000017||Gonzo_442||12-22-2000||06:19 PM||||I feel sorry for people like you. You do not, can not, or perhaps will not understand what it means.<P>I guess i am one of those that people term a 'gun nut'.<P>In every instance of violence in which guns actually played a factor, you will note the determining factor is a person. A firearm is an object. It does not think. It does not feel. It cannot do anything other than function.<P>Each person can look back upon thier life, and recall times they enjoyed. Hopefully, they were times spent with family and friends. Some of the best moments of my life were spent with my father and grandfather, in various pursuits. Including the use and care of firearms.<P>A person raised with the proper values and respect would not commit the crimes that the media sees fit to drum up, further inciting copy cat crimes.<P>FWIW, i carry. Every day. I have the right, and you do not have the right to stop me. I have the right to protect myself and my family. Any one who feels different, please feel free to explain.<P>As far as hunting, if you have never gone hunting, please do not presume to tell me what i should or should not do. Currently in the state of PA, officials are releasing more tags (you need a tag to take an animal) in an effort to reduce the deer population. The current population will outstrip the winter food supply at the peak of winter, leaving the whole herd to starve until spring. As Cheever noted, lack of natural predators lets most game animals run rampant. This is not the first, nor the last time, and certainly not the only area to face such a problem. <BR>Further, you probably enjoy working on your car, as i'm sure most of us do. I also happen to enjoy hunting. Since it neither takes money from your pocket, nor harms you in any way, please leave me be.<P>Now i would like to turn the floor over to an esteemed gentleman from the past, Mr. Samuel Adams.<BR>"If you love wealth more than liberty, the tranquility of servitude better than the animating contest of freedom, depart from us in peace.  We ask not your counsel, nor your arms.  Crouch down and lick the hand that feeds you. May your chains rest lightly upon you and may posterity forget that you were our countrymen."<P>--<BR>Mat<P>Sorry if this is a bit long, but you brought up the topic, and it is a touchy one. You are entitled to your opinion, and i am entitled to mine. Thank you.||||reg||1
Z||000018||StuGood||12-22-2000||07:28 PM||||Just some rambling thoughts...<P>I don't own any guns.  Don't have any want/need for them.  If I develop an interest in them, I will have to accept that there are certain responsibilities that accompany gun ownership - just as there are responsibilities associated with owning an automobile.  I <B>do</B> very much respect people's rights to own them, enjoy them, and to use them responsibly.  Many people do.  <P>People who don't know anything about them   (like me) have another responsibility: not to mess with them.  I suppose a <B>few</B>  should outright be prohibited from owning/using them, if they demonstrate an inability or refusal to act responsibly - kind of like being banned from driving.  <P>As has been said many times, guns don't kill people; people do.  If guns were banned, these same people would be using cars, rocks, their hands, etc., so banning weapons isn't a solution.  People who repeatedly demonstrate irresponsible behavior get restrictions placed upon them, up to and including being locked away.   <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000019||Will||12-23-2000||03:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>All an armed society will do is make frivolous killing feel normal. At the same time, banning guns will have the same affect as banning rats. Illegal, but still there. The only real changes in society come from the commitment to change, not laws to force behavour. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I disagree with your comment about an armed society.<BR>     I agree with your last statement, however. Society is composed of people. For society to change, the people who comprise it must change. Just as passing laws against SUVs won't keep <I>baka</I> from getting behind the wheel, banning guns won't stop criminal behaviour.<BR>     Just as improvements in driver education and a reform of the misleadingly named traffic "justice" system (don't get me started on this one!) are the appropriate actions to take to keep <I>baka</I> off the road, a return to the values of individual responsibility and the thorough application of the Golden Rule is what is necessary to curb criminal behaviour.<BR>     Are cars responsible for highway deaths? Obviously not. Stupid drivers are. I don't think anyone who proposes banning cars has thought the matter through very well.<BR>     I find it ridiculous that firearms are blamed for the lack of self-control, lack of respect for fellow men, lack of forethought, and self-destructive nature of the modern American populace.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Phil||12-23-2000||06:10 AM||||Is our new Aussie member reading this? I hear that since they went for an almost total gun ban home invasions have been on a BIG increase. Just trying to verify a rumor.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Philphine||12-23-2000||10:18 AM||||i'm next to youngest in a fairly wide spaced (bettween childrens ages) family. i was told my father used to do some hunting but my oldest brother accidentaly shot himself in the foot so my father got rid of his guns (i don't think i was even born then). didn't grow up around them and got myself an air rifle at about 18. my father's only advice was don't ever aim at someone unless you really mean to shoot them.<P>joined the national guard and shoot every 3 years for m16 qualification. thats my only experience with actual firearms. never developed an interest  ||||reg||1
Z||000022||terryk||12-23-2000||01:03 PM||||Hi Will,<P>Maybe I wasn't clear (not unusual)....<P>1. I don't want guns banned.<BR>2. I think a short waiting period is fine.<BR>3. I don't think an armed society (packing 24/7) will make things safer for people.  <BR>4. Things will change when people want it to change.<P>However, I fully support the lawn-dart ban....Sheesh<P>||||reg||1
